<p class="lt-chem-55887">Coupling constants are sometimes written <sup>n</sup>J to denote the number of bonds (n) between the coupled nuclei. Alternatively, they are written as <em data-effect="italics">J</em>(H-H) or <em data-effect="italics">J</em><sub>HH</sub> to indicate the coupling is between two hydrogen atoms. Thus, a coupling constant between a phosphorous atom and a hydrogen would be written as <em data-effect="italics">J</em>(P-H) or <em data-effect="italics">J</em><sub>PH</sub>. Coupling constants are calculated empirically by measuring the distance between the peaks of a multiplet, and are expressed in Hz.</p>

