<p class="lt-chem-55887">The peak intensities that are weak in Figure \(\PageIndex{34}\) depend on the level of functionalization and for highly functionalized SWNTs, those peaks are not weak. The peak intensity for aliphatic carbons can be enhanced as the substituents get modified by attaching to other molecules with aliphatic carbons. Thus, the peak intensities can be used to quantify the level of functionalization.</p>

</div></div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Analytical_Chemistry/Physical_Methods_in_Chemistry_and_Nano_Science_(Barron)/04:_Chemical_Speciation/4.07:_NMR_Spectroscopy" class="mt-section" id="section_48"><span id="13C_NMR_of_Functionalized_Graphene"></span><h4 class="lt-chem-55887 editable"><sup>13</sup>C NMR of Functionalized Graphene</h4>

<p class="lt-chem-55887">Graphene is a single layer of sp<sup>2</sup> carbons, which exhibits a benzene-like structure. Functionalization of graphene sheets results in converting some of the sp<sup>2</sup> carbons to sp<sup>3</sup>. The peak for the sp<sup>2</sup> carbons of graphene shows a peak at around 140 ppm. It has been reported that fluorinated graphene produces an sp<sup>3</sup>peak at around 82 ppm. It has also been reported for graphite oxide (GO), which contains &ndash;OH and epoxy substituents, to have peaks at around 60 and 70 ppm for the epoxy and the &ndash;OH substituents, respectively. There are chances for similar peaks to appear for graphene oxide. Table \(\PageIndex{6}\) summarizes these results.</p>

